  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The multiplicity of the root $x=3$ in the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+11x-6$ is{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "Since $x=3$ is a root of $p(x),$ the factor theorem tells us that $(x-3)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBecause $(x-3)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-3)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $-6$ | $11$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $-9$ | $6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-3$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{p(x)}{x-3}={x}^{2}-3x+2,\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\np(x)=(x-3)({x}^{2}-3x+2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =(x-3)(x-1)(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x-3)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=3$ has multiplicity $1.$",
